J5 c97f PUSHED as GERRIT CHANGE 1015 — https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/1015 (stack: 1010 J2 <- 1011 J4 <- 1013 J3 <- 1015 J5).
Full suite: 7595 passed / 29 skipped / 118 deselected / 0 FAILED in 27m48s. Unit tier 5768 passed. make lint + make typecheck green. docker build succeeds.
Commit: 8 files (5 new harness files, the new unit-tier structural test, the workflow, the Makefile). Trailers verified, 50/72 passed, verify-commit-ticket OK.

TIERING TELEMETRY (whole epic): four code stories implemented by STANDARD/mid-tier (sonnet) subagents, each named explicitly on dispatch. Attempts to green the happy path: J2 unreported-but-passed-first-validation, J4 = 2, J3 = 1, J5 = structural-only (live half impossible locally). ZERO tier escalations across the epic. The pattern that made this work: the brief carried every constraint the behavioral oracle cannot see — module-size cap, lint rules, and for J5 the entire body of hard-won operational fact (both mandatory flags, the two verified-dead property names, the digest, loopback, TTY, 8.x-only). Cheap tier was never warranted; strong tier was never needed.

SCORECARD ON THE HELD-OUT DISCIPLINE, honestly: across J2/J3/J4/J5 the held-out oracles found essentially NO implementation defects on first validation — the implementers satisfied tests they could not read. What the discipline actually caught was SIX defects in MY OWN test code, four of them tests that PASSED while proving nothing:
 1. teeth test with package strings one level too deep (broke relative-import arithmetic);
 2. wrong accessor (backend.resolve_assignee vs backend.outbound.resolve_assignee) — 7 tests red, my fault not the implementer's;
 3. conflating 'has a body' with 'duplicates logic' — counted a sanctioned delegation as a violation;
 4. substring 'truncated' in fitted instead of endswith — caught by the COMPLETION VERIFIER, would have passed a marker stranded mid-string;
 5. regex over a compose file matching the anti-pattern quoted in its own warning COMMENT — false positive on correct config;
 6. 'flag in text' on a Dockerfile whose comments name the flag — hollow, and hollow BECAUSE a sibling AC required that documentation.
Numbers 4-6 are the same failure mode: an assertion that cannot fail. Mutation testing found 5 and 6; a gate found 4. That is the strongest argument in this run for keeping mutation checks mandatory rather than optional — a green oracle is not evidence that the oracle works.
